          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಅಯ್ಯೋ (ayyō) — \"oh no!\", \"oh dear!\". You hear it everywhere in the south when something goes wrong."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಅಯ್ಯೋ (ayyō) is native Dravidian (DEDR 196). DEDR lists ayyō, a cry of grief, wonder or pity, in the part of that entry that holds the cries, beside Tamil aiyō and Telugu ayyō. It compares Sanskrit aye, a cry of surprise."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The sound of a small disaster."
